    "case_id": "270",
    "case_summary": "A woman in her 40s with a medical history of infective endocarditis and hepatitis C secondary to ongoing intravenous drug use presented to the emergency department with severe back pain. She had recently undergone tricuspid valve replacement with a 29-mm bioprosthetic Carpentier-Edwards valve complicated by transient third-degree heart block. Two days after pacemaker placement, she experienced episodes of light-headedness and chest pain.\n\nBlood pressure was 94/60 mm Hg. Heart rates were between 150 and 190 beats per minute.\n\n- Electrocardiogram findings:    Image Title: Figure 1. Electrocardiogram During Tachycardia    Image Description: Shows P waves upright in leads II, III, and aVF with sinus rate of 96 beats per minute. Grouped beating pattern with 1 P wave producing 2 QRS complexes, creating a regularly irregular rhythm.      - Prior Imaging:    Transesophageal echocardiography revealed pulmonic valve vegetations and severe tricuspid valve regurgitation secondary to a flail leaflet.",
